Eq. (4) characterizes the inherent preferences of the i-th NIA determined by pumping rate γp,i and spontaneously occurring decisions with decay rate γe,i. Notably, if the external information pumping rate is high enough and γp,i > γe,i, the user becomes initially excited, i.e. σ0,i > 0 and is more likely to support decision Se. Otherwise, for γp,i < γe,i, the users represent a passive environment and tend to support decision Sg. At γp,i = γe,i the users have no preferences and σ0,i = 0. We can assume that in this case, on average users remain uncertain in their decision. In the first order of perturbation theory, we can assume that population imbalance σi is given by Eq. (4). Now for the given population imbalance, σ0,i, we can monitor NIA’s excitation level and s-field behavior, which may be recognized as a limit of social impact occurring within the DIS and carried out by AIAs through the network,
